{
  "description": "issue #660 (Sprites Epic Gate): `fill` is named among the per-turtle commands at spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:109 (\"`pen_up`, `pen_down`, `set_color`, `set_width`, `fill`, and `stamp` use the current turtle's pen and shape state\"), so the MUST at :113 applies to it: \"When multiple turtles are addressed by `tell`, a turtle command applies once for each addressed turtle. Implementations MUST produce trace events with the appropriate turtle identity\". :a is given pen colour `red` and :b `blue` via scoped `ask`; the single `fill` under `tell [ :a :b ]` then fans out into TWO `fill` events, each stamped with its own acting `turtle_id` (1 then 2) and each carrying THAT turtle's own pen colour in its payload — `{color: \"red\"}` for id 1, `{color: \"blue\"}` for id 2. The deliberately DIFFERENT colours are the point: a `fill` payload carries per-turtle pen colour (spec/rendering.md's \"Fill\": \"The fill color is the current pen color\", and its \"Color\": \"A segment, fill, or stamp captures the color at the moment its event is applied\"), so unlike a command whose per-turtle correctness rides only on the shared fan-out mechanism, `fill` has its own observable that a shared-state bug would collapse to one colour. Both colours are from the normative 11-colour palette (spec/commands.md's \"Colors\"). Every change of the addressed set — here from `ask`/`tell` — also emits the addressing `primitive` event carrying `addressing: { addressed_turtle_ids, current_turtle_id }`, so a consumer can identify the addressed turtle set (issue #766, spec/rendering.md:193); `ask`/`each` emit one again when the previous set is restored, on every exit path. Paired with fill-each-per-turtle-color, which proves the same per-turtle colour resolution under `each`'s one-turtle-at-a-time narrowing rather than this statement-level fan-out.",
